WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.980
Speaker 1: I've been very careful when we've
been talking about George W. Bush during the

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:09.580
Donald Trump era not to participate in the
rehabilitation of George W. Bush's image.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.440
And this is one of the dangers of not talking
about former presidents, which we discussed

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.870
a few weeks ago, which is they end up being
allowed either implicitly or explicitly to

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:23.090
sort of rewrite their own history and legacy
as presidents of the United States.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:29.230
In George W. Bush's case, his legacy is not
taking the 9/11 threat seriously enough to

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:35.700
start with, more importantly, reacting to
the 9/11 attacks by invading a country that

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.700
didn't do it.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:43.480
The country of Iraq declaring mission accomplished
years early, staying in a total quagmire of

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:49.390
a situation while hemorrhaging money and blowing
up the deficit, all to get us nothing while

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.810
committing war crimes.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.600
That's a bad legacy.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:56.750
And I haven't even mentioned the Bush administration's
response to Hurricane Katrina, which is another

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.790
complete and total fiasco.

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:05.199
And yes, at the same time as an individual
compared to Donald Trump, Bush's less wacky.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:07.090
He's less obviously unhinged.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:08.640
He sounds more reasonable.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.730
He seems to relate to people just on an individual
level in a more normal way than Donald Trump.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:20.140
But George W. Bush has partially rehabbed
his image by becoming friends with Michelle

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.130
Obama and by being friends with Ellen DeGeneres
and painting portraits of veterans.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:30.340
And now he's sort of like a friendly, older
past president who just wants everybody to

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.480
get along.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:38.050
So the point is, I've been careful when we
say, wow, even George W. Bush realizes that

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:42.909
this thing Trump did or that thing Trump did
is terrible to understand the context of the

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:45.120
horrible legacy of George W. Bush.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.300
Now, that gets us to this morning.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:53.260
This morning, George W. Bush appeared on the
Today show and he actually laid out a pretty

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.860
cogent case against the direction of the modern
Republican Party since Bush left office through

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.810
the Obama administration and during Donald
Trump's one term presidency.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.490
And he is completely correct.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.600
He arguably doesn't even go far enough.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.390
Let's listen and then we'll talk about it.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:16.580
Speaker 3: I would describe it as isolationist,
protectionist and to a certain extent, nativist,

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.580
disappointed.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.950
Well, that's not exactly my vision.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:21.950
But you know what?

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.560
I'm just an old guy they put out to pasture.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.580
So just a simple painter.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.020
A simple
Speaker 1: painter.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.290
So let's take this in pieces.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.879
I mean, first of all, is what George W. Bush
is saying, correct?

00:02:32.879 --> 00:02:33.980
It is correct.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:40.850
The Republican Party has gone since Bush left
more fully to the I don't even know that I'd

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.310
call it isolationism and protectionism.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.590
I'd call it nationalism.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:49.350
And yes, nativism and even nativism doesn't
really go far enough.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:55.110
It's really more it's almost like a xenophobia
that's very popular in white nationalist circles.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.920
It's it goes beyond nativist.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.920
So Bush is right and he probably doesn't go
far enough.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:06.910
Now, it is true or better said, is it true
that this is not Bush's vision for the party,

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:10.660
as he says, and that it wasn't Bush's vision
when he was president?

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.500
Well, yes, sort of.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.210
But the truth is more complicated.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:15.210
It's not.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.580
The Republican Party has now gone nativist
xenophobe.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.980
And Bush wasn't at all that when he was president
of the United States.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:31.099
The reality is that Bush's GOP was maybe more
indifferent to the nativism and they played

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:32.709
coy with the nativism.

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:39.270
And while they were less cartoonishly talking
about it in the way Trump did, they allowed

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.270
it.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:43.410
And they even had many people that were part
of the administration that were very much

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.260
of that mindset.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.280
Bush personally like his Bush personally,
a nativist xenophobe, like what he now criticizes

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.489
about the Republican Party.

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:53.489
Probably not.

00:03:53.489 --> 00:03:54.489
I don't really know.

00:03:54.489 --> 00:03:56.510
But it's sort of like his Trump personally
anti-Semitic.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.510
We talked before.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.780
Well, you know, Trump has a Jewish daughter
by conversion.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.110
Ivanka and his son in law, Jared Kushner,
and Trump has Jewish grandkids.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.400
And all this stuff doesn't really matter.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.630
Trump coalesced the antisemite, so his personal
view isn't super relevant to me.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:16.359
Whatever George W. Bush's personal view of
the nativist, xenophobic wing of the Republican

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:21.579
Party is, Bush's presidency certainly didn't
call out that wing of the Republican Party,

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:28.280
and it allowed that wing to grow into what
it became during Obama and ultimately led

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.280
to Donald Trump.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.129
So with all of these issues, it's more complicated
than what Bush believes and it's more nuanced

00:04:35.129 --> 00:04:40.520
than Bush wasn't at all on the isolationist,
nativist GOP side.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:46.070
It's more like Bush probably calculated that
the Republican Party was going in that direction.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.569
Even if it wasn't his thing.

00:04:47.569 --> 00:04:53.129
He found a way to placate that wing without
being as cartoonishly welcoming of it as Donald

00:04:53.129 --> 00:04:54.240
Trump was.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.969
So what this gets us to is like, how should
we react when George W. Bush criticizes Trump

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:00.029
and now is.

00:05:00.029 --> 00:05:04.279
Criticizing the modern Republican Party, is
Bush a hero for calling it out, or is he a

00:05:04.279 --> 00:05:06.809
foe who's pretending not to have been a part
of it?

00:05:06.809 --> 00:05:08.490
Neither life is more complicated.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.009
It's not always black and white.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:14.699
The reaction for me is Bush was a disaster.

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:20.069
But even Bush realizes that the Republican
Party sends him and under Trump is an even

00:05:20.069 --> 00:05:23.270
bigger disaster, but he's also partially responsible
for it.

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.270
They all are right.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:31.120
I mean, you know what the right during the
civil rights era, into the Vietnam era, into

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.740
Reagan, into Gingrich and then so on, into
Bush.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:35.740
Right.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:38.840
I mean, it's all part of what got that party
to what it is today.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.409
And you don't have to praise George W. Bush
to realize that.

00:05:42.409 --> 00:05:47.759
But you can also recognize the role that he
either implicitly or explicitly or by negligence

00:05:47.759 --> 00:05:50.669
had in getting the party to where it is today.

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:54.490
That being said, it's I wonder.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:06:00.039
How much of the modern Republican electorate,
which is mostly behind Donald Trump care,

00:06:00.039 --> 00:06:02.270
is what George W. Bush says at this point
in time?

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:03.270
I don't know.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.359
The answer to that remains to be seen

